  "notes": "USAGE:\n{背|そむ}ける is transitive — the thing turned away (face, eyes, body) takes を. The most common pattern is {目|め}を{背|そむ}ける (to avert one's eyes) or {顔|かお}を{背|そむ}ける (to turn one's face away).\n\nDistinguish from {背|そむ}く (intransitive, godan verb), which means \"to go against\" or \"to betray\" (e.g., {命令|めいれい}に{背|そむ}く, to defy an order). {背|そむ}ける is the ichidan transitive verb meaning to physically turn away.\n\nConjugation: ichidan verb ({背|そむ}けない / {背|そむ}けます / {背|そむ}けて).\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {目|め}を{背|そむ}ける: to avert one's eyes, to look away\n- {顔|かお}を{背|そむ}ける: to turn one's face away\n- {現実|げんじつ}から{目|め}を{背|そむ}ける: to turn a blind eye to reality",
